  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $P(n):$ $\\sqrt{n}>0⇒{n}^{3}<0.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n\nII. The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n\nIII. $∀n\\in N,\\,P(n).$",
  "worked_solution": "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$\\sqrt{n}>0$\" is $N,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${n}^{3}<0$\" is $∅.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{N}\\cup ∅=∅\\cup ∅=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne N.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ does *not* coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
